WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.559
Factoring reverses distribution by recognizing a common multiplicative structure

00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:11.954
Both terms contain x, so x squared plus four x becomes x times x plus four

00:00:11.954 --> 00:00:17.500
Expanding the product again verifies that the original expression has been preserved.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.984
For a monic quadratic, look for two numbers with the required sum and product

00:00:23.984 --> 00:00:32.500
One and four add to five and multiply to four, so they produce the middle and constant terms when these binomials are expanded.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.580
A factored equation equal to zero splits into simpler cases

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:45.897
A product of real numbers is zero only if at least one factor is zero, yielding inputs negative one and negative four

00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:50.433
This step is not valid for a sum merely equal to zero.

00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:55.123
Factoring also reveals cancellable factors in a quotient

00:00:55.123 --> 00:01:03.596
The difference of squares contains x minus two in its entire numerator, so it cancels for nonzero x minus two

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:07.833
The original exclusion remains, leaving a hole at input two.
